VeridianOS: predicate parser: a string list value is the list, not the '{' token

Real bug, found while looking at the bison-generated predicate_parser.c (not
reported by GCC). The rule

    string_list: '{' string_list_rec '}' { $$ = $<ptr>1; }

returns the semantic value of the '{' token, which the lexer sets to 0
(yylval->name = 0), instead of the list built by string_list_rec ($2). A
predicate with a string-list value, e.g. "Foo.bar == {'a', 'b'}", therefore
handed a null value to PredicateParse_newAtom(), which dereferences it
(*val) and crashes; the list itself was leaked. Use $<ptr>2.

The regenerated parser differs from the as-built one only in that action
(yyvsp[-2] -> yyvsp[-1]).

diff -ru a/src/solid/devices/predicate_parser.y b/src/solid/devices/predicate_parser.y
--- a/src/solid/devices/predicate_parser.y	2026-10-08 17:14:46.685435507 -0400
+++ b/src/solid/devices/predicate_parser.y	2026-10-08 17:14:46.697598250 -0400
@@ -85,7 +85,7 @@
      | VAL_FLOAT { $$ = PredicateParse_newDoubleValue( $<vald>1 ); }
      | string_list { $$ = $<ptr>1; }
 
-string_list: '{' string_list_rec '}' { $$ = $<ptr>1; }
+string_list: '{' string_list_rec '}' { $$ = $<ptr>2; }
 
 string_list_rec: /* empty */ { $$ = PredicateParse_newEmptyStringListValue(); }
                | VAL_STRING { $$ = PredicateParse_newStringListValue( $<ptr>1 ); }
